// NEGATIVE TEST — a tuple has at least two elements (N4-12).
//
// `(e)` is grouping, in this corpus and in every language its readers know, so a
// one-element tuple would have to be `(e,)` — a spelling whose only purpose is
// to disambiguate against grouping, decided by a comma a reader can miss.
//
// The normative production (`grammar.ebnf:428`, `"(" expression { "," expression }
// [ "," ] ")"`) PERMITS the one-element form. Refusing it is a documented SUBSET
// of the spec rather than a contradiction: the requirement row this fixture
// serves is "Tuples (A, B) with construction and .0 access", the two-or-more
// spelling, and a form whose meaning turns on a trailing comma is the kind of
// thing this project refuses to add before somebody needs it.
fn main() {
    let p = (1,);
    print_int(p.0);
}
